window.docContents[247].resolve({"/Types-and-expressions/Objects-and-pointers/#Core-C______--Types-and-expressions--Objects-and-pointers":{"contents":"The literal nullptr has the internal type , compatible with every pointer type and with no other. Its value is .\n\nThe expression new C() allocates one location per field of , each with the default value of its type, then the record itself, tagged with the class, and evaluates to a pointer to the record. Its type is . The empty parentheses are the whole argument list for a class without a constructor. A class with a constructor takes its arguments in those parentheses, .\n\nThe dereference *e denotes the location the pointer holds. When the pointer is  the result is error, where C++17 leaves the dereference undefined.\n\nThe field access e.f denotes the location of the field  in the record that  denotes, and e->f abbreviates (*e).f, so a null pointer is error. The type of the field comes from the class table.\n\nReading *e, e.f or e->f as a value is reading the content of the location it denotes, one rule for the three forms. Three typing rules give the value the type of the location. An element v[i] of a vector is a use of the library, .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Types and expressions","header":"3.6. Objects and pointers","id":"/Types-and-expressions/Objects-and-pointers/#Core-C______--Types-and-expressions--Objects-and-pointers"}});